# Part of Cloudberry's isolation2_schedule (src/test/isolation2), as the port
# runs it on a coordinator and three segments: every test of the schedule
# but four of append-optimized tables' own (below), in the schedule's
# order -- distributed transactions, distributed snapshots, locks
# and the global deadlock detector (M3), FTS and mirrors (M4), resource
# queues and memory accounting (M6), the tools -- a node recovered
# elsewhere, the standby coordinator promoted, removed, made again and
# waited for, a node copied with pg_basebackup (M7) -- directory tables on a
# cluster (M8), append-optimized tables under sessions side by side -- their
# segment files, VACUUM's compaction, cursors, snapshots and indexes (M5) --
# and the rest: a session of a node's own, the dispatcher and its gangs,
# indexes, VACUUM and ANALYZE, the bitmap index, and Cloudberry's faults in
# PostgreSQL's own code, which the tests' build has as injection points
# (../../docker/patches).
#
#   run   the test runs here, in both passes; cloudberry/ holds a difference
#         reviewed and kept, where one pass has one.  A third word names the
#         group it runs in, "main" when there is none: each group has a
#         cluster of its own, and the groups of a pass run side by side, each
#         in the schedule's order.  A group name ending in "*" is every group
#         that name begins, so gdd/prepare, which turns the global deadlock
#         detector on, and gdd/end, which turns it off, run first and last in
#         each group of GDD tests; and several names with commas between
#         them are each of them, so disable_autovacuum runs in main and
#         first in the two groups of append-optimized tables' tests.  The
#         groups are cut by time: each GDD test waits out the detector's
#         five-second period at least once.  The group named standby has a
#         standby coordinator too, the group whose name begins "mirrors" a
#         mirror for each segment besides, and one whose name begins "uao"
#         the connections of Cloudberry's demo cluster.
#   skip  it needs what the port does not have yet: the rest of the line says
#         what
#
# A test whose name begins "port/" is the port's own, beside Cloudberry's
# (sql/port and expected/port here): what the port does in a place of
# Cloudberry's the schedule's tests do not reach.
#
# The schedule's tests of append-optimized tables' own are 133: uao/, each
# of whose 56 files Cloudberry's pg_regress makes a test of a row table and
# one of a column table, x_row and x_column (run.sh), and 21 others.  The
# 110 of them that ask nothing the port lacks are listed: 47 of uao/'s
# files, in the groups uao_row and uao_column, after disable_autovacuum as
# the schedule has them, with the three tests the schedule runs between the
# two; ao_partition_lock, bitmap_index_ao_sparse and the reindex/ tests of
# ao and aoco tables in main, beside their heap twins.  The other 23 want a
# fault, a setting or a view of Cloudberry's, or read its own layout of an
# append-optimized table's auxiliary relations.  Nine of them run, with what
# gp_ao and gp_core have of those since: uao/alter_while_vacuum2,
# ao_unique_index_vacuum, fast_analyze and max_concurrency2, of a row and of
# a column table, and ao_index_build_progress.  Ten are skipped for the
# layout they read, five of them with a test of the port's own beside them
# that is Cloudberry's test for gp_ao's layout: port/ao_blkdir,
# port/ao_unique_index, port/aocs_unique_index and
# port/uao/vacuum_cleanup_row and _column, the last two one file made a row
# table's test and a column table's, as Cloudberry's are (run.sh, input/port
# and output/port here).  And four are not listed yet: uao/brin and
# uao/brin_chain, of a row and of a column table, which read BRIN's revmap
# page by page.  Its FTS tests are M4's, and are: the ones
# that run are in the groups mirrors1 to
# mirrors3, whose clusters have a mirror for each segment -- three, so that
# they run side by side -- M7's in mirrors4, whose standby they change, and
# dtx_recovery_wait_lsn in mirrors5, whose recovery process waits a minute
# for FTS to let a mirror go, and fts_errors in mirrors6, which fails two
# contents over and recovers both, a minute and a half on its own.
# gpexpand_catalog_lock, whose sessions hold every other session's catalog
# changes, is in a group of its own, as the schedule keeps it; so are
# instr_in_shmem_setup and instr_in_shmem_terminate, metrics, the first of
# which restarts the cluster to turn query metrics on, and the tests that
# PANIC a node, crash.  And the rest of the tests that restart nodes or copy
# one: terminate_in_gang_creation in mirrors2, the pg_basebackup tests in
# mirrors3, segwalrep/master_wal_switch in standby, the bitmap index's
# crash recovery in mirrors7, and pg_rewind_fail_missing_xlog in mirrors8,
# which sets wal_keep_size and wal_recycle, restarts the cluster, and fails
# two contents over and recovers them twice each.
